    "workflow": "Research and Due Diligence",
    "full_prompt": "The source materials provided for this request follow.\n\nEntity: THE BOEING COMPANY\nINCOME STATEMENT (USD, annual 10-K values)\nConcept (as reported) | FY end 2021-12-31 | FY end 2022-12-31 | FY end 2023-12-31 | FY end 2024-12-31 | FY end 2025-12-31\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nRevenues | 62,286,000,000 | 66,608,000,000 | 77,794,000,000 | 66,517,000,000 | 89,463,000,000\nCostOfRevenue | 59,237,000,000 | 63,078,000,000 | 70,070,000,000 | 68,508,000,000 | 85,174,000,000\nGrossProfit | 3,049,000,000 | 3,530,000,000 | 7,724,000,000 | -1,991,000,000 | 4,289,000,000\nOperatingIncomeLoss | -2,870,000,000 | -3,519,000,000 | -773,000,000 | -10,707,000,000 | 4,281,000,000\nNetIncomeLoss | -4,202,000,000 | -4,935,000,000 | -2,222,000,000 | -11,817,000,000 | 2,235,000,000\nIncomeTaxExpenseBenefit | -743,000,000 | 31,000,000 | 237,000,000 | -381,000,000 | 397,000,000\nResearchAndDevelopmentExpense | 2,249,000,000 | 2,852,000,000 | 3,377,000,000 | 3,812,000,000 | 3,615,000,000\nEarningsPerShareBasic (USD/shares) | -7.15 | -8.3 | -3.67 | -18.36 | 2.49\n\n---\n\nEntity: THE BOEING COMPANY\nCASH FLOW (USD, annual 10-K values)\nConcept (as reported) | FY end 2021-12-31 | FY end 2022-12-31 | FY end 2023-12-31 | FY end 2024-12-31 | FY end 2025-12-31\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nNetCashProvidedByUsedInOperatingActivities | -3,416,000,000 | 3,512,000,000 | 5,960,000,000 | -12,080,000,000 | 1,065,000,000\nNetCashProvidedByUsedInInvestingActivities | 9,324,000,000 | 4,370,000,000 | -2,437,000,000 | -11,973,000,000 | 499,000,000\nNetCashProvidedByUsedInFinancingActivities | -5,600,000,000 | -1,266,000,000 | -5,487,000,000 | 25,209,000,000 | -3,763,000,000\nPaymentsToAcquirePropertyPlantAndEquipment | 980,000,000 | 1,222,000,000 | 1,527,000,000 | 2,230,000,000 | 2,942,000,000\n\nUsing these materials, complete the following request.\n\nUsing only the supplied annual income-statement and cash-flow extracts, prepare an investment-committee brief comparing The Boeing Company’s profitability, liquidity evidence and cash generation. Focus on FY ended 2025-12-31 versus FY ended 2024-12-31, with FY ended 2023-12-31 as a benchmark. The central question is whether the latest earnings performance is matched by internally generated cash, and how far these extracts support a liquidity conclusion.\n\nUse compact exhibits followed by an analytical conclusion. Present monetary amounts in USD millions, preserve signs, and calculate from unrounded inputs; show percentages and percentage-point changes to one decimal place. For each input, identify the supplying statement, reported concept and fiscal endpoint, using a source legend if helpful. Distinguish reported amounts from analyst calculations.\n\nShow revenue, gross profit, operating income or loss, net income or loss, operating cash flow, and payments to acquire property, plant and equipment for all three years. Calculate gross, operating and net margins using Revenues as the denominator. Define an analyst cash-generation measure as NetCashProvidedByUsedInOperatingActivities less PaymentsToAcquirePropertyPlantAndEquipment, and label it as a free-cash-flow proxy rather than a reported company measure. Also calculate, as a signed earnings-to-cash gap rather than a reconciliation of its causes, operating cash flow minus NetIncomeLoss.\n\nQuantify the 2025-versus-2024 revenue growth rate, changes in each margin in percentage points, and dollar changes in net income, operating cash flow and the free-cash-flow proxy. Use dollar changes rather than percentage growth rates for those earnings and cash measures when the comparison base is negative. Compare the 2025 margins, operating cash flow and free-cash-flow proxy with 2023, explicitly indicating above, equal to or below the benchmark. Use these results and the earnings-to-cash gaps to assess whether profitability and cash generation tell a consistent story; do not infer working-capital movements, noncash charges or recurring earnings quality from the selected lines alone.\n\nDevelop the liquidity discussion from two distinct flow-based tests. First, classify operating cash flow as covering reported property, plant and equipment payments when the free-cash-flow proxy is nonnegative, and not covering them when it is negative. Second, calculate operating plus investing cash flow and then the sum of all three reported cash-flow activity totals for each year. Explain how the reported financing inflow or outflow changes the pre-financing balance. Keep this broader test separate from the capital-spending coverage test: do not deduct property, plant and equipment payments again from the activity totals, infer particular financing or investing transactions, or call the three-activity sum the fully reconciled change in cash.\n\nConclude which dimensions strengthened or weakened year over year and which remain above or below 2023, grounding the funding assessment in the preceding calculations. Separately address whether closing cash resources and the current ratio can be established. If inputs are absent, mark the result not determinable and identify the specific missing balances needed; annual cash flows must not be substituted for balance-sheet liquidity, and a flow-based improvement must not be presented as proof of adequate liquidity.",
