    "full_prompt": "The source materials provided for this request follow.\n\nEntity: Salesforce, Inc.\nINCOME STATEMENT (USD, annual 10-K values)\nConcept (as reported) | FY end 2022-01-31 | FY end 2023-01-31 | FY end 2024-01-31 | FY end 2025-01-31 | FY end 2026-01-31\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nRevenueFromContractWithCustomerExcludingAssessedTax | 26,492,000,000 | 31,352,000,000 | 34,857,000,000 | 37,895,000,000 | 41,525,000,000\nCostOfGoodsAndServicesSold | 7,026,000,000 | 8,360,000,000 | 8,541,000,000 | 8,643,000,000 | 9,270,000,000\nGrossProfit | 19,466,000,000 | 22,992,000,000 | 26,316,000,000 | 29,252,000,000 | 32,255,000,000\nOperatingIncomeLoss | 548,000,000 | 1,030,000,000 | 5,011,000,000 | 7,205,000,000 | 8,331,000,000\nNetIncomeLoss | 1,444,000,000 | 208,000,000 | 4,136,000,000 | 6,197,000,000 | 7,457,000,000\nIncomeTaxExpenseBenefit | 88,000,000 | 452,000,000 | 814,000,000 | 1,241,000,000 | 2,063,000,000\nResearchAndDevelopmentExpense | 4,465,000,000 | 5,055,000,000 | 4,906,000,000 | 5,493,000,000 | 5,993,000,000\nEarningsPerShareBasic (USD/shares) | 1.51 | 0.21 | 4.25 | 6.44 | 7.85\n\n---\n\nEntity: Salesforce, Inc.\nBALANCE SHEET (USD, annual 10-K values)\nConcept (as reported) | FY end 2022-01-31 | FY end 2023-01-31 | FY end 2024-01-31 | FY end 2025-01-31 | FY end 2026-01-31\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nAssets | 95,209,000,000 | 98,849,000,000 | 99,823,000,000 | 102,928,000,000 | 112,305,000,000\nLiabilities | 37,078,000,000 | 40,490,000,000 | 40,177,000,000 | 41,755,000,000 | 53,163,000,000\nStockholdersEquity | 58,131,000,000 | 58,359,000,000 | 59,646,000,000 | 61,173,000,000 | 59,142,000,000\nAssetsCurrent | 22,850,000,000 | 26,395,000,000 | 29,074,000,000 | 29,727,000,000 | 28,222,000,000\nLiabilitiesCurrent | 21,788,000,000 | 25,891,000,000 | 26,631,000,000 | 27,980,000,000 | 37,118,000,000\nCashAndCashEquivalentsAtCarryingValue | 5,464,000,000 | 7,016,000,000 | 8,472,000,000 | 8,848,000,000 | 7,327,000,000\nAccountsReceivableNetCurrent | 9,739,000,000 | 10,755,000,000 | 11,414,000,000 | 11,945,000,000 | 14,339,000,000\nLongTermDebtNoncurrent | 10,592,000,000 | 9,419,000,000 | 8,427,000,000 | 8,433,000,000 | 10,439,000,000\nLongTermDebt | 10,596,000,000 | 10,601,000,000 | 9,426,000,000 | 8,433,000,000 | 14,439,000,000\n\n---\n\nEntity: Salesforce, Inc.\nCASH FLOW (USD, annual 10-K values)\nConcept (as reported) | FY end 2022-01-31 | FY end 2023-01-31 | FY end 2024-01-31 | FY end 2025-01-31 | FY end 2026-01-31\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nNetCashProvidedByUsedInOperatingActivities | 6,000,000,000 | 7,111,000,000 | 10,234,000,000 | 13,092,000,000 | 14,996,000,000\nNetCashProvidedByUsedInInvestingActivities | -14,536,000,000 | -1,989,000,000 | -1,327,000,000 | -3,163,000,000 | -8,590,000,000\nNetCashProvidedByUsedInFinancingActivities | 7,838,000,000 | -3,562,000,000 | -7,477,000,000 | -9,429,000,000 | -8,079,000,000\nPaymentsToAcquirePropertyPlantAndEquipment | 717,000,000 | 798,000,000 | 736,000,000 | 658,000,000 | 594,000,000\n\nUsing these materials, complete the following request.\n\nPrepare a financial scenario-comparison decision brief for an internal treasury review of Salesforce, Inc. The decision is whether the latest earnings and cash-generation evidence supports continuing the recent aggregate investing and financing cash-use pattern or favors a more cash-preserving posture. Use only the three supplied financial tables. Show dollar amounts in USD billions, retain cash-flow signs, identify fiscal endpoints explicitly, and provide formulas and source concept names for derived measures.\n\nEstablish the historical baseline for the fiscal years ended 2025-01-31 and 2026-01-31. Compare revenue growth, operating margin, operating cash flow relative to net income, and operating cash flow less PaymentsToAcquirePropertyPlantAndEquipment. Use the appropriate preceding fiscal year for each revenue-growth calculation. Label the last measure as an analytical cash-generation proxy, not a reported free-cash-flow line or unrestricted cash available for deployment. Alongside those results, compare year-end cash, working capital and the current ratio. Explain whether the earnings, cash-generation and current-liquidity measures give a consistent decision signal, distinguishing observed relationships from unsupported causal explanations.\n\nBefore modeling the alternatives, check the historical cash bridge for each of those two years. Sum the signed operating, investing and financing cash flows and compare that sum with the change in CashAndCashEquivalentsAtCarryingValue between the corresponding opening and closing fiscal endpoints. Define the residual as the balance-sheet cash change minus the three-category cash-flow sum. Report any residual without assigning it to foreign exchange, restricted cash or another missing line, and explain what this limitation means for the scenario cash estimates.\n\nThen compare two explicitly hypothetical one-year cash-use sensitivities, both starting with reported cash at 2026-01-31 and holding operating cash flow at the amount reported for the year then ended. In the recent-pattern case, repeat that year's signed investing and financing cash flows. In the historical-benchmark case, substitute the signed investing and financing cash flows reported for the year ended 2025-01-31. These substitutions are analytical assumptions, not forecasts, reconstructed historical results or evidence that management can recreate either mix. Do not forecast earnings or a complete balance sheet.\n\nFor each case, calculate the modeled net cash movement and indicative ending cash before unprovided reconciling items. Show whether the assumed operating cash flow covers the combined net investing and financing outflows. If a case would reduce opening cash, quantify the aggregate reduction in those net outflows needed to preserve it; do not allocate that reduction to transactions absent from the tables. Do not subtract capital expenditure again after including the reported net investing cash flow, and do not carry a historical bridge residual into either case as a forecast assumption.\n\nConclude with a conditional recommendation grounded in the scenario difference, the historical profitability and cash-conversion results, and the latest current-liquidity position. Separate the arithmetic ranking of the cases from your judgment about an executable treasury response. Identify the decision-critical evidence still needed about cash availability and the commitments or transactions underlying investing and financing cash flows; describe these as proposed follow-up evidence, not source-mandated documents. Do not treat positive modeled ending cash alone as proof of adequate liquidity or the ability to meet all obligations.",
